cdot ⟨3,-4,4⟩ & =23.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083941/state.json" "file_sha256" "c3772d6b854140e60bbd312b06204f6ca9a31bcadad5f7e63336617b8bfce89e"} {"question" "q-127927" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083941/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "469aeca0be34dfbee839b187a5a6b65023627281cfe038dbd6e81074ac320b31"} {"question" "q-127927" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083941/state.json" "file_sha256" "c3772d6b854140e60bbd312b06204f6ca9a31bcadad5f7e63336617b8bfce89e"} {"question" "q-127927" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "r\\cdot ⟨-4,-4,3⟩=11"] ["math" "r\\cdot ⟨-4,3,4⟩=12"] ["math" "r\\cdot ⟨3,-4,4⟩=23"] ["math" "r\\cdot ⟨3,4,4⟩=23"] ["math" "r\\cdot ⟨4,-3,4⟩=11"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083941/state.json" "file_sha256" "c3772d6b854140e60bbd312b06204f6ca9a31bcadad5f7e63336617b8bfce89e"} {"question" "q-127927" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "r\\cdot ⟨3,-4,4⟩=23"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083941/state.json" "file_sha256" "c3772d6b854140e60bbd312b06204f6ca9a31bcadad5f7e63336617b8bfce89e"} {"question" "e-4932" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find an equation of the plane that contains the point $P(-3,2,1)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}= \\langle 0, 2, 0 \\rangle.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083941/example-4932.json" "file_sha256" "48f4f90a8b30b9d0b5794ce85853d58194d5a2a7b8ae3fde46d811c298cc0a79"} {"question" "e-4932" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -3,2,1 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 0,2,0 \\rangle$ is normal to the plane.\n\nThe equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}$ can be given as\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{r} - \\mathbf{p}) \\cdot \\mathbf{n} = 0,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{r}$ is the position vector of any point on the plane, and $\\mathbf p$ is the position vector of $P.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,2,0⟩ & =⟨-3,2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0,2,0⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,2,0⟩ & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083941/example-4932.json" "file_sha256" "48f4f90a8b30b9d0b5794ce85853d58194d5a2a7b8ae3fde46d811c298cc0a79"} {"question" "e-4933" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A nonzero vector $\\langle 0,\\, \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{b}, \\: \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{c} \\rangle$ is parallel to the plane $\\mathbf{r} \\cdot \\langle 2, -1, 4 \\rangle  = 3.$ What is the value of $\\dfrac{b}{c}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083941/example-4933.json" "file_sha256" "e0c18de60d0e06c0e252ff35465a6df0a54d5129950caa3d996d2269f76cff45"} {"question" "e-4933" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need a vector $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle t_1,t_2,t_3 \\rangle$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation $\\mathbf{r} \\cdot \\langle 2, -1, 4 \\rangle  = 3$ of the plane, we get the normal vector $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 2, -1, 4 \\rangle.$ So, $\\mathbf{v} \\perp \\mathbf{n}$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,-1,4⟩ & =0 \\\\ 2{t}_{1}-{t}_{2}+4{t}_{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}_{2} & =2{t}_{1}+4{t}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}=\\langle t_1, 2t_1 + 4t_3, t_3 \\rangle,\n$$\n\nwhere $t_1,t_3 \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$ \n\nWe are told that the first component of $\\mathbf{v}$ equals $0.$ So, we must set $t_1=0.$ \n\nSince $\\mathbf{v}$ is nonzero, we can pick any $t_3 \\neq 0.$ For example, setting $t_3=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} = \\langle 0,\\, \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{4}, \\, \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{1} \\rangle.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\dfrac{b}{c} = 4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083941/example-4933.json" "file_sha256" "e0c18de60d0e06c0e252ff35465a6df0a54d5129950caa3d996d2269f76cff45"} {"question" "e-4935" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $Q(\\sqrt{2},0,-5)$ and is perpendicular to the line\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3 \\\\ y=-1+\\sqrt{3}t \\\\ z=5-t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083941/example-4935.json" "file_sha256" "2e7d5a1b81eb0c92963aa55d41ea6645dbdc5e24b93d0a8245254779c2cd5e1c"} {"question" "e-4935" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the position vector of $Q$ is $\\mathbf{q}=\\langle \\sqrt{2},0,-5 \\rangle.$ Converting the parametric equations of the line to vector form, we get\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083941/assets/3c1e2e4034bcbc860f548935ca7305bc8f141c56ba14f821fd1aa6cb2b53f8d9.png)\n\nThe direction vector of the line is $\\mathbf{v}=\\langle  0 , \\sqrt{3} , -1\\rangle,$ so a normal vector to the plane is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\mathbf{v}=\\langle  0 , \\sqrt{3} , -1 \\rangle.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,\\sqrt{3},-1⟩ & =⟨\\sqrt{2},0,-5⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\sqrt{3},-1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,\\sqrt{3},-1⟩ & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083941/example-4935.json" "file_sha256" "2e7d5a1b81eb0c92963aa55d41ea6645dbdc5e24b93d0a8245254779c2cd5e1c"}] "usage" {"e-4935" [] "e-4933" [] "q-33319" [] "q-127927" [] "q-32971" [] "q-33317" [] "q-127873" [] "q-70894" [] "e-4932" [] "q-127948" [] "q-33312" [] "q-70892" [] "q-33027" []} "basis" 2880 "retractions" [[17592186120149 :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that contains the point $P(2,-5, 3)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n = ⟨ - 1, 2,-3⟩$."] [17592186119569 :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vector of $Q$ is $\\mathbf{q}=\\langle \\sqrt{2},0,-5 \\rangle.$ Converting the parametric equations of the line to vector form, we get\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/assets/4c70e51d85874e5a241a5d9b35b147500d4c306187c5e0a1d15a48a9aef07706.png)\n\nThe direction vector of the line is $\\mathbf{v}=\\langle  0 , \\sqrt{3} , -1\\rangle,$ so a normal vector to the plane is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\mathbf{v}=\\langle  0 , \\sqrt{3} , -1 \\rangle.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,\\sqrt{3},-1⟩ & =⟨\\sqrt{2},0,-5⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\sqrt{3},-1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,\\sqrt{3},-1⟩ & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186117415 17592186117416 17592186117417 17592186117418 17592186117419 17592186117607 17592186117608 17592186117609 17592186117610 17592186117611 17592186316919 17592186316920 17592186316921 17592186316922 17592186316923 17592186316933 17592186316934 175921863